      The dataset is collected from scenarios where dual-contact occurs sequentially. The three types of observations are referred to as (a)
      single-contact, (b) transition dual-contact, and (c) steady dual-contact.
    </div>
  </div>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Key idea: Contact Diffusion Model (CDM)</h2>
  <p>
    We address the uncertainty of localization by employing a diffusion model, known for effectively handling multi-modal distributions. To the best
    of our knowledge, this is the first attempt at contact localization using generative models. Consequently, the proposed method does not require a
    difficult-to-tune threshold to distinguish between single-contact and dual-contact scenarios. Inspired by PF-based algorithms, we modify the
    diffusion model to operate recursively using past model outputs, which helps reduce multi-modality in cases where dual-contact occurs
    sequentially. When the last single contact occurs at time $T_s$, the posterior is expressed as
  </p>
  <div aria-label="Equation 1" class="project-equation" role="region" tabindex="0">
    $$ p(\mathbf{r}_t \mid \mathbf{O}_{t-T:t},\, \mathbf{r}_{T_s},\, \mathcal{S}) $$
  </div>
  <p>Moreover, CDM does not rely on simplified surface representations by employing the signed distance field (SDF) of the robot surface.</p>
  <div class="project-media-card">
    <a aria-label="Open full image: CDM architecture" href="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/network_structure.webp' | relative_url }}"
      ><img alt="CDM architecture" decoding="async" loading="lazy" src="{{ '/assets/img/research/cdm/network_structure.webp' | relative_url }}"
    ></a>
    <div class="card-caption">
      In the inner loop, the denoiser sequentially eliminates noise from the white Gaussian noise (from $k = K$ to 1). To incorporate the surface
      constraints on the points, the pre-trained SDF network for each link surface is utilized. In the outer loop, the contact state classifier
      determines whether to use the diffusion output from the previous time step. On the right, the structure of the denoiser network is shown.
    </div>
  </div>
  <h2 class="project-section-title">Technical components</h2>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">1. Dataset</h3>
  <p>
    Each sample $\mathbf{X}_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n_p \times 3}$ is a duplication of $\mathbf{r}_t$, where $n_p$ is the number of points ($n_p = 64$). The
    reasons are: (i) the dimension of $\mathbf{r}_t$ changes depending on the number of contacts $n_c$, making it difficult to handle both single and
    dual-contact cases with a single model, and (ii) with $n_c$ sampled points, it is challenging to capture multi-modality (e.g., when $n_c = 2$ and
    the posterior has three modes).
  </p>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">2. Historical diffusion result conditioning</h3>
  <p>
    The key idea is that $\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{T_s}$ is sampled from the first contact point in the dataset: the diffusion process is applied to the
    first contact point, using a randomly sampled diffusion step $k_h \sim \mathcal{U}(1, K)$. Intuitively, in the dual-contact case, the denoiser
    network is trained to find the contact points, whether or not the first contact point is given (i.e., whether the sampled $k_h$ is close to 1 or
    not). This also allows null-conditioning, so CDM can operate even when there is no information about the first contact. In the inference process,
    the contact state classifier $g_\psi$ is utilized to update $\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{T_s}$ only in single-contact cases.
  </p>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">3. Surface constraints conditioning</h3>
  <p>
    To guide the generated samples to be located on the robot surfaces, the denoiser network is conditioned on the signed distance field (SDF) of all
    link surfaces. For each link and point, the SDF network outputs the shortest distance to the link and a unit vector toward the link. The denoiser
    is similar to U-Net, where the convolution layers are replaced by multi-layer perceptrons (MLPs), and incorporates a conditioning structure using
    FiLM and Fourier-feature mapping.
  </p>
