    <div class="project-callout-box">
      <h3>Ablation studies</h3>
      <ul class="project-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Historical diffusion result conditioning (Experiment #3):</strong> for all metrics, CDM-his shows more than twice the improvement
          compared to CDM-null (M-RMSE 0.62 cm vs. 1.33 cm; C-RMSE-1/2 1.63/2.53 cm vs. 3.89/6.09 cm).
        </li>
        <li>
          The proposed method shows about a 40% performance improvement over our previous PF-based algorithm, MCP-EP (M-RMSE 1.08 cm), using realistic
          observations including sensor noise and the convergence time of the DOB. Strictly speaking, the metric used in the PF-based algorithm
          differs from M-RMSE, as it evaluates the localization error of a single solution.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>SDF conditioning (Experiment #2):</strong> the average distance between the generated samples and the robot surfaces is 0.29 cm with
          SDF, whereas without SDF it is 0.89 cm.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 class="project-subsection-title">Direct sim-to-real transfer (Experiment #4)</h3>
  <p>
    All experiments are conducted with a 7-DOF Kinova Gen3 robot arm equipped with JTSs and an F/T sensor (ATI Mini85) at the base. The dataset was
    collected with MuJoCo, and noise is added to the JTSs and base F/T sensor measurements. To obtain the locations of the true contact points,
    labeled markers were attached to the robot. Single and dual contact scenarios were conducted 100 times each, and every 20 experiments, the robot
    configuration was randomly set. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first quantitative evaluation of dual-contact scenarios in the real
    world.
  </p>
